<!--
    GH-1922 (kuna): "Calls to an in-image memcpy keep only their first
    arguments (Betaflight, DWARF)"
    https://github.com/Noelo-Lab/kuna/issues/1922

    Reduced from Betaflight (Cortex-M4, gcc -O2, DWARF), where calls to the
    firmware's own newlib memcpy printed one or two of their three arguments
    whenever the caller's DWARF prototype was applied.  Hand-written Thumb-2,
    assembled with clang for thumbv7em and linked at 0x10000:

      mcpy          newlib-shaped copy: reads r0, r1 and r2, writes r3 first.
      cp4           reads r0 and r1 only.
      sink          reads r0.
      copy_profile  void copy_profile(unsigned char dst, unsigned char src):
                    push {r3, lr}; movs r2,#0xa4; ldr r3,=tbl;
                    mla r1,r2,r1,r3; mla r0,r2,r0,r3; bl mcpy
                    (pidCopyProfile: the size also feeds both multiplies).
      burst         void burst(unsigned char a, unsigned char *data,
                    unsigned char len): push {r3, r4, r7, lr}; uses len for
                    a later call and passes data and len on untouched
                    (sx1280WriteCommandBurst).
      one           void one(int *d, int *s): push {r3, lr}; movs r2,#4;
                    add r1,r2; bl cp4.  Control: cp4 does not read r2.

    Stage model: P4 active-input-trial scoring.  The size register is
    refused by onlyOpUse (it has another use), and the callee-read rule
    (kuna_calleereadarg.rs) declined to add it back because each caller's
    entry stores r3, its last argument register, only to save it (gcc's
    `push {r3, lr}` alignment pad), which the rule reads as a possible
    variadic register-save area.  The calls printed mcpy(a,b) and mcpy(a).
    A caller whose locked prototype states a fixed parameter list has no
    such area, so the rule now applies to it; the parse lines stand in for
    the DWARF prototypes.
-->
